{"nodes": [{"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_churn___retention", "label": "Churn & Retention", "file_type": "document", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "document", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_the_funnel_leaks_at_three_points___diagnose_which_before_fixing", "label": "The funnel leaks at three points \u2014 diagnose which before fixing", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: The funnel leaks at three points \u2014 diagnose which before fixing", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The three most common SaaS funnel holes for Brazilian founders: (1) cancel in month 1\u20132 \u2192 onboarding missing or LP promised the wrong thing (brought off-ICP users); the first is a product problem (shorten path to value), the second marketing (tighten the promise). (2) Many trials, few paid \u2192 paywall at the wrong moment or a broken value ladder. (3) Many visits, few signups \u2192 confused ICP and/or generic value proposition \u2014 but check the acquisition source first; wrong traffic can't be saved by copy."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_churn_and_payback_are_one_problem__measured_in_two_places", "label": "Churn and payback are one problem, measured in two places", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Churn and payback are one problem, measured in two places", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Churn and payback look like two problems (often with different owners) but are the same one \u2014 both decided in the user's first session, in the gap between entering and feeling the product works."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_switching_cost_is_what_turns_months_of_ltv_into_years", "label": "Switching cost is what turns months of LTV into years", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Switching cost is what turns months of LTV into years", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The same product, designed differently, yields months vs. years of LTV. Switching cost = the effort a user *perceives* in leaving; low switching cost means thin history and an easy exit."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_tell_the_existing_base_about_upgrades_before_they_want_to_leave", "label": "Tell the existing base about upgrades before they want to leave", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Tell the existing base about upgrades before they want to leave", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Reactive improvement communication is a sneaky churn vector: if you only market new versions to cold traffic to avoid cannibalizing the old product, your base assumes the old version is the ceiling and leaves when a competitor looks better."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_design_the_cancel_screen___it_s_your_last_conversation__not_a_form", "label": "Design the cancel screen \u2014 it's your last conversation, not a form", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Design the cancel screen \u2014 it's your last conversation, not a form", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The cancellation screen is the most ignored yet one of the most important pages in the product; treating it as a bureaucratic form wastes your final chance to retain."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_churn_starts_on_the_landing_page__not_at_cancel", "label": "Churn starts on the landing page, not at cancel", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Churn starts on the landing page, not at cancel", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "If the LP promises one thing and the product delivers another, you create an expectation debt that charges interest every day the user thinks \"this isn't what I expected\" \u2014 and the disappointment is pre-programmed even if the product is excellent."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_anchor_one_time_job_products_to_a_recurring_life_event", "label": "Anchor one-time-job products to a recurring life event", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Anchor one-time-job products to a recurring life event", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A product hired to solve a one-off problem generates structural churn: a user who loves it and still cancels isn't unhappy \u2014 they finished the job they came to do, and there was no continuous value to bring them back."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_treat_support_volume_as_a_design_problem__not_a_staffing_one", "label": "Treat support volume as a design problem, not a staffing one", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Treat support volume as a design problem, not a staffing one", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "What looks like a support problem is usually a design problem \u2014 you cut ticket volume during onboarding itself, with an interface that answers questions before they're asked. Low-ticket digital products generate up to 3x more support than conventional tickets."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_strip_the_jargon_before_you_blame_onboarding", "label": "Strip the jargon before you blame onboarding", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Strip the jargon before you blame onboarding", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Churn that looks like a product problem is often a language problem: a technical founder writes product and sales copy in jargon, the ICP buys on a leap of faith, never perceives value, accumulates small disappointments, and cancels \u2014 looking like a missing feature on the dashboard."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_retention_is_raising_the_cost_of_leaving_day_after_day___ifood_s_behavioral_lock_in", "label": "Retention is raising the cost of leaving day after day \u2014 iFood's behavioral lock-in", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Retention is raising the cost of leaving day after day \u2014 iFood's behavioral lock-in", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "iFood isn't just a business model \u2014 it's Brazil's most sophisticated behavioral lock-in. Each layer (delivery, data, credit, card) raises the psychological and operational cost of leaving: sunk-cost effect plus ecosystem dependency. The restaurant doesn't stay because iFood is good; it stays because leaving got too expensive. Retention isn't a feature \u2014 it's the accumulated cost of exit."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_churn_math__restructured_onboarding_can_preserve_r_4_8k_mrr_from_an_existing_base", "label": "Churn math: restructured onboarding can preserve R$4.8k MRR from an existing base", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Churn math: restructured onboarding can preserve R$4.8k MRR from an existing base", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "6% churn on 900 users at R$300 ARPU is 54 cancellations, or R$16.2k MRR, leaving each month. Restructured onboarding (contextual welcome + empty state + activation checklist) cut cancellations by ~30%: about 16 accounts retained, preserving R$4.8k MRR \u2014 a R$57.6k ARR run-rate or R$52.8k over the post's stated 11-month average lifetime. No new traffic, no new feature."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_engineer_addiction_like_a_game_so_cs_isn_t_a_churn_tax", "label": "Engineer addiction like a game so CS isn't a churn tax", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: Engineer addiction like a game so CS isn't a churn tax", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Customer Success is the tax you pay for a non-addictive product \u2014 if you need an army of CSMs to stop cancellations, the product failed. Your real competitor isn't another startup; it's boredom, and boredom has infinite CAC. The CNPJ buying your SaaS is the same brain that plays Candy Crush; reward neuroscience is identical. Win retention across three game-design phases."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention_the_3_biggest_churn_reasons___and_the_fix_for_each", "label": "The 3 biggest churn reasons \u2014 and the fix for each", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "section: The 3 biggest churn reasons \u2014 and the fix for each", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Most churn traces to three causes, each with a distinct fix. (1) Bad onboarding \u2192 gradual guided immersion to what users value most, a Zeigarnik progress bar (incompleteness nags), and targeted tooltips on unexplored features. (2) Missing perceived value \u2192 show user ROI in a simple dashboard (Grammarly shows words helped), promote feature discovery continuously, build community. (3) Hard/confusing interface \u2192 fix via a proper product design project."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention__richardrx___2026_08_05", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-08-05", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-08-05", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2085056354360729949"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention__richardrx___2026_06_30", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-06-30", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-06-30", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2071931705573748896"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention__richardrx___2026_06_23", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-06-23", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-06-23", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2069382946214080985"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention__richardrx___2026_05_19", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-05-19", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-05-19", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2056715097796411514"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention__richardrx___2026_05_13", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-05-13", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-05-13", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2054562119962501186"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention__richardrx___2026_04_29", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-04-29", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/churn-and-retention.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-04-29", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2049568514311172355"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_churn_and_retention__richardrx___2026_04_24", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 20